{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffHujGZRZUAVz3ndFHRM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote 8 test statistics for the incumbent `tally_span_personal_ideal` into `iter_2/critique/test_stats/`. Each returns one finite float on the real responses (5,120 rows) in under 0.02 s per call, well within the 5 s limit. I haven't run the posterior-predictive check; the pipeline runs it next.\n\nThe incumbent looks at one thing: how far a sequence's running heads-minus-tails count swings. The statistics test what that misses:\n\n| Statistic | What it tests | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `heads_preference_mirror_pairs` | In exact H↔T mirror pairs (e.g. HHHH vs TTTT), how often people pick the more-heads one. The model treats heads and tails the same, so it predicts about 0.5. | 0.569 |\n| `participant_heads_preference_variance` | How much people differ in preferring heads-heavy sequences. The model has no per-person heads preference. | 0.0049 |\n| `shorter_longest_run_at_equal_span` | When both sequences swing equally, how often people pick the one with the shorter streak. | 0.475 |\n| `balanced_count_at_equal_span` | When both sequences swing equally, how often people pick the one with heads and tails closer to equal. | 0.475 |\n| `more_distinct_triplets_at_equal_span` | When both sequences swing equally, how often people pick the one with more varied three-flip patterns. | 0.491 |\n| `more_switches_at_near_equal_span` | When the swings differ by at most one step, how often people pick the sequence that switches between H and T more often. | 0.711 |\n| `consensus_extremity_close_span_pairs` | How strongly people agree on pairs the model sees as nearly equivalent. | 0.217 |\n| `late_minus_early_streak_avoidance` | Whether avoiding streaks gets stronger or weaker over the session. The model assumes it stays constant. | −0.022 |\n\nTwo things to keep in mind:\n- **Small samples:** the three \"both sequences swing equally\" statistics rest on only a handful of distinct pairs each, so their p-values will be noisy.\n- **Two statistics replaced:** a first version had one switch-rate statistic that used almost the same pairs as the balance statistic, so I swapped it for `more_switches_at_near_equal_span`. I also narrowed the heads statistic to exact mirror pairs, where the heads preference is clearest (e.g. HHHH was chosen over TTTT in 75% and 58% of trials, depending on which side it was shown on). Both changes came from looking at which pairs each statistic covers, not from p-values, since none have been computed yet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2042,"cache_read_input_tokens":58991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2042,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0600f96b-984c-4a3c-84f1-be1f493705e4","uuid":"a101a5d2-ffd4-4fb2-830b-e1ec3ee8fdd6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T12:19:54.507Z","request_id":"req_011CffHuirG2gniGoRDRkeK3"}
